<p>Sarah Mullally is being installed on Wednesday at Canterbury Cathedral as the 106th archbishop of Canterbury, becoming the first woman to hold the role in the office’s nearly 1,500-year history. The enthronement marks the symbolic start of her ministry and places her at the spiritual center of the Anglican communion, which includes about 85 million people worldwide. Mullally takes on the post at a difficult moment for the Church of England, as it grapples with safeguarding failures, internal divisions, and broader questions about its role in modern public life. Supporters have welcomed her appointment as a milestone for women’s leadership in the church, while some factions in the global Anglican community oppose a woman leading the Church of England — a sign of the tensions she will have to navigate from the start.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/UXsgNZAtVUbVH1woVkwaHXDm4b16JiOfMi-g-O50ulkxrNyiMl1JeHcJCU3eW_ja-lcIcoHbpDPiPiCrJdqvSvkYiGpb5BNmE640ZAlzx1lZiIgiCIme0TW8BfSbiXxsM8vN8QCg6Q-0hujaobA3YFwgEPTnhhNfm4ZLE5_R1522QacMLxrYctuuTcEB7O7y8uMIgfwpA0WzhyQ7Z3A' alt='The ceremony in Canterbury on Wednesday is the symbolic start of Sarah Mullally’s ministry, after she was legally confirmed as archbishop at St. Paul’s Cathedral in London in January.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Two-step transition: Mullally’s appointment became legally effective in January, while Wednesday’s enthronement marks the public, ceremonial start of her ministry.</li><li>Recent pathway: The Church of England did not open the episcopacy to women until 2014, so a woman could only reach Canterbury after that change.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Supporters of women’s leadership: Backers have celebrated Mullally’s selection as a historic breakthrough, since she is the first woman to lead the Church of England. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/25/world/europe/sarah-mullally-archbishop-of-canterbury-church-of-england.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>Some Anglican factions: Parts of the global Anglican Church have denounced her appointment, reflecting continuing opposition to a woman serving in the role. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/25/world/europe/sarah-mullally-archbishop-of-canterbury-church-of-england.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>Church observers: Some analysts see Mullally’s arrival as a test of how much influence the archbishop of Canterbury still carries, as the church faces internal strains and questions about its place in modern British public life. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/25/wednesday-briefing-can-sarah-mullally-steer-the-church-of-england-back-into-safer-waters'>The Guardian</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/25/wednesday-briefing-can-sarah-mullally-steer-the-church-of-england-back-into-safer-waters'>Wednesday briefing: ​Can Sarah Mullally steer the Church of England back into safer waters?</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/25/world/europe/sarah-mullally-archbishop-of-canterbury-church-of-england.html'>Sarah Mullally, the First Female Archbishop of Canterbury, is About to Be Enthroned</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/europe/mullally-to-be-installed-as-first-female-archbishop-of-canterbury'>Sarah Mullally to be installed as first female Archbishop of Canterbury</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/cancer-nurse-turned-archbishop-celebrates-election-woman-lead-131386477'>Cancer nurse turned archbishop celebrates election as first woman to lead Church of England</a> - abcnews.com</li></ul>